# Sets

A set holds each value once. Braces around values write one, and a repeated
value is dropped:

```hql
{"novel", "essay", "novel", "poetry"}
```

```text
{"novel", "essay", "poetry"}
```

<!-- run: check -->
```hql
{"novel", "essay", "novel", "poetry"}
```

```text
Set<String>
```

A set has no positions. It prints its elements in some order, but the order is
not part of its value, and two sets with the same elements are equal:

```hql
{1, 2, 3} == {3, 2, 1}
```

```text
true
```

`{}` is the empty set, a `Set<Never>`. A declared type gives it an element
type: `seen : Set<String> = {}`. `{:}`, with a colon, is an empty `Data`
record instead; [[02-data-types/sets-data-and-maps|Sets, Data and maps]] tells
the brace forms apart.

## Building a set

`Set(xs)` builds a set from any collection, and `xs | Set` is the same call
written as a step. This is the usual way to find the distinct values of a
list:

```hql
genres = ["novel", "essay", "novel", "poetry", "novel"]
genres | Set | size
```

```text
3
```

## Membership

`contains` asks whether a value is in a set. Here the set holds the books
already read, and `filter` keeps the titles that are not in it:

```hql
read = {"Dune", "Emma"}
["Dune", "Ubik", "Emma", "Solaris"] | filter(t => not read.contains(t))
```

```text
["Ubik", "Solaris"]
```

## Union, intersection and difference

`union` returns the values in either set, `intersection` the values in both,
and `difference` the values in the first and not in the second:

```hql
ada = {"Dune", "Emma", "Ubik"}
bob = {"Emma", "Solaris"}
[union(ada, bob), intersection(ada, bob), difference(ada, bob)]
```

```text
[{"Dune", "Emma", "Ubik", "Solaris"}, {"Emma"}, {"Dune", "Ubik"}]
```

Each also works as a step, `ada | union(bob)`. `union_all` returns the union
of a whole collection of sets.

## Steps over a set

`map` and `filter` over a set return a set. Because a set holds each value
once, `map` can return a smaller set than it was given:

```hql
{"Dune", "Emma", "Ubik"} | map(t => t.size())
```

```text
{4}
```

The same `map` over a list keeps one result per element:

```hql
["Dune", "Emma", "Ubik"] | map(t => t.size())
```

```text
[4, 4, 4]
```

## No positions

A set has no positions, so it has no first element and no element at
position `1`. The checker rejects a step that needs positions:

```hql
{3, 1, 2} | drop(1)
```

```text
check error[type-mismatch]: `drop` needs values in a stable order, and Set<Int> has none; sort it first
 --> <expression>:1:13
  |
1 | {3, 1, 2} | drop(1)
  |             ^^^^^^^
```

`sort` gives the elements an order, and its result is a list:

```hql
{3, 1, 2} | sort | drop(1)
```

```text
[2, 3]
```

`min`, `max`, `sum` and the other reductions do not depend on order, and work
on a set directly.
